{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:premortem","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:5bb4fe7c3f81f111c2de8c2cf40ed6d6c7b46104c1f5c203a71e99bac16eb3ae","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.1},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.2}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"THE SINGLE STRONGEST SHARED ASSUMPTION ACROSS ALL THREE WAVE-1 SEATS, AND MY ATTACK ON IT. All three converged on P50 1.8-1.9% from three supposedly independent lenses, and all three got there the same way: subtract 2A's aging drags from a near-current growth rate. That is a DOUBLE COUNT. The IMF WEO Apr-2025 Ch.2 figures are 2025-2050 averages against a NO-AGING counterfactual, and China's observed ~4.5-5% print is already produced by an economy with a working-age population falling since 2013 and total population falling since 2022. Panelist 2 made the error auditable by publishing its implication — a no-aging China counterfactual of 5.4%/yr, i.e. the claim that today's print contains no realized drag at all. The drag is also back-loaded: worker/retiree 4.4 -> 2.8 by the mid-2030s, with the steep part in the 2040s. Incremental 2026 -> 2032-36 China drag: -1.0 to -1.3 pp, not -1.8 pp realized.\n\nSECOND ATTACK, WHICH NO SEAT MADE: WHAT RESOLVES THIS QUESTION. CQ3 resolves on measured PPP real GDP from official statistics channelled through IMF WEO. China's official real-GDP series is a smoothed administrative object with a documented tendency to print near target. Wave 1 forecast TRUE Chinese growth and will be scored on PRINTED Chinese growth. Even a true 3.0% plausibly prints 4.0-4.5%. I take measured China 2032-36 P50 = 3.6%.\n\nPPP WEIGHTS. OECD-38 ~$78T, China ~$42T (2025 PPP); China 0.35 at the start, drifting to 0.37-0.38 by the 2034 midpoint on its own faster growth. w_China = 0.37, w_OECD = 0.63.\n\nOECD-38 LEG. Locked 0.6%/yr per capita + ~0.25%/yr population = +0.85 pp. AI TFP lift, probability-weighted on AI-Q6's own regime means (0.162x0.75 + 0.472x0.35 + 0.365x0.07) = +0.31, which I take at +0.30 net of a small Baumol haircut. CAPITAL DEEPENING: +0.20 pp, NOT the +0.07 panelist 2 booked. He halved it for AI-Q4 impairment, which is a book-value event that does not reduce capital services or measured output; and the measurement channel runs the other way — the data-centre and grid build IS measured investment, and AI-Q3's implied ~8.7%/yr electricity growth is measured utilities value added. Damages/electricity-cost drag -0.10. OECD-38 = 0.85 + 0.30 + 0.20 - 0.10 = 1.25%/yr.\n\nCOUPLED P50 = 0.63 x 1.25 + 0.37 x 3.6 = 0.79 + 1.33 = 2.12 -> P50 = 2.1%/yr. China supplies 1.33 pp of it: the arithmetic the brief demands still says the answer is mostly a China-measurement question, but it points UP from the Wave-1 centre, not down.\n\nJONES/BAUMOL: I accept panelist 2's CES arithmetic and his honest finding, which cuts against the Wave-1 conclusion he drew from it — with sigma=0.5 and an 0.8/0.2 split the weakest-link LEVEL cap is hard but binds in the 2040s-50s, costing only ~0.15-0.30 pp by 2036. A cap that does not bind inside the window cannot justify a 1.8% P50. What binds by 2036 is diffusion and the physical build, and both are already inside my +0.30 TFP term.\n\nATTACK ON THE SIMULATION CLOSURE (panelist 2 read bounds off it). Verified in this repo: production.ts sets gamma = 0.55 on useful energy (paramMeta range 0.05-0.70) against mainstream cost-share logic of 0.05-0.08, so model GDP is substantially an energy-throughput function and its own electrification/datacenter build manufactures growth — biasing every reading UP, which is why its baseline prints 2.95% and tech-breakthrough 3.68%. The demographic path tracks UN WPP LOW (~18% below medium by 2100), and over THIS window that is growth-FLATTERING, not damaging: post-2025 births do not reach the labour force until the 2040s while low fertility lightens young dependency now — so the low variant biases a 2036 reading UP as well. scripts/tetlock-sim-adapter.ts sets OECD_PROXY = ['us','oecd-ex-us'], disclosed as excluding Japan, Korea, Turkiye, Mexico, Chile, Colombia and Costa Rica: it drops two of the three most-aged OECD economies (UP bias) AND all five high-inflation members (which is why the model is doubly useless for CQ2, in a direction nobody noticed). Net: every bound read off this model is biased UP on growth, and panelist 2 was right to refuse point estimates — but his four reasons for rejecting it are also four reasons his own downward adjustments were partly double-counted against figures already net of realized aging.\n\nBAND. P10 1.0: capex bust landing in-window plus a China property/pension accident printing sub-2.5% plus two sub-zero OECD years. P90 3.2: measured China 4.5% with OECD at 1.9% (0.63x1.9 + 0.37x4.5 = 2.86) plus favourable weight drift and a genuine productivity window. Mild left skew (P50-P10 = 1.1 vs P90-P50 = 1.1, with the left tail fatter inside the band).","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.604Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"by_when\":\"IMF WEO October 2029 edition\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ3 P50 falls from 2.1% to ~1.8% and P90 to ~2.9%; CQ6-A drops from 25 to ~14 with the mass going to B (+8) and C (+3). This kills my measured-vs-true-China argument, which carries ~0.3 pp of my CQ3 and the bulk of my A.\",\"signpost\":\"IMF WEO prints combined 2030 and 2031 China real GDP growth below 3.5%/yr (not a forecast - the realized/estimate line for those years), i.e. the official series stops printing near 4.5%.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Q4 2029 OECD CPI release\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ2 falls from 48 to ~32, CQ6-C from 27 to ~18, CQ6-D rises from 6 to ~13. This falsifies the PPP-composition argument that is the single load-bearing plank of my CQ2 and of my unusually low D.\",\"signpost\":\"OECD-wide GDP-weighted core inflation (OECD's own published aggregate) averages below 2.30%/yr for four consecutive quarters, with Turkiye's core in single digits and Mexico's below 3.5%.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ5 falls from 30 to ~15, i.e. back through the Wave-1 centre. The MOVE level-scaling argument is my whole edge on CQ5; a sub-90 three-year average at 4%+ nominal yields refutes it empirically rather than theoretically.\",\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE three-year average over 2027-2029 prints below 90 while the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal yield averages above 3.75%.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 conjunct 1 collapses from 0.45 to ~0.20 and CQ1 falls from 6 to ~3; CQ3's left tail fattens (P10 to ~0.7) and CQ6-D rises ~5 points. It also tests the Okun-favourable demographic-drift claim all four seats relied on.\",\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 harmonized unemployment prints above 6.5% in any single month, or above 6.0% for two consecutive quarters.\"} | {\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ4 P50 falls from 2.3% to ~1.4% and P90 to ~2.8%; CQ6-D rises from 6 to ~14 and the asset matrix's long-nominal-bond row re-rates sharply. This falsifies my cut of the repression wedge to -0.10 and my non-US-convergence term, the two places I depart from the Wave-1 CQ4 centre.\",\"signpost\":\"G7 GDP-weighted 10y sovereign real yield (10y nominal minus 10y inflation swap) averages below 1.00% for twelve consecutive months, with the 10y JGB real yield still below 0.50%.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 2 premortem seat, effort 'growth'."}}